<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William IV of the United Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William IV was king of the United Kingdom and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> from 1830 to 1837, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> who had served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. His reign saw the Reform Act of 1832 and the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-saxe-meiningen">Queen Adelaide</a> gave her name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the short-lived Province of Queen Adelaide in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-iv-of-the-united-kingdom.jpg" alt="William IV of the United Kingdom" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Archer Shee · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_IV_in_1833_by_Shee_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born on 21 August 1765 at Buckingham House, the third child and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Queen Charlotte</a>. With two elder brothers, George, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, and Prince Frederick, he was not expected to inherit the Crown. He was educated by private tutors at Richmond and Kew Palace, and at thirteen joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> as a midshipman. He was present at the Battle of Cape St Vincent in 1780, and was arrested with his shipmates after a drunken brawl in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> before being hastily released. He was later nicknamed the &quot;Sailor King&quot;.</p><p>He ceased active service in 1790 and was never given a command during the Napoleonic Wars; his rank as admiral from 1798 was purely nominal. From 1791 to 1811 he lived with the Irish actress Dorothea Bland, known as Mrs Jordan, and they had ten children. In 1818 he married Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-saxe-meiningen">Adelaide of Saxe-Meiningen</a>. As Lord High Admiral from 1827 he clashed repeatedly with his Council, and the king asked for his resignation in 1828 after he put to sea with a squadron for ten days without saying where.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>George IV died on 26 June 1830 and William succeeded him at the age of 64. He discouraged pomp, and walked unaccompanied through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and Brighton. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a> said he did more business with William in ten minutes than with George IV in as many days. The general election of 1830 brought the Whigs under Lord Grey to power, and Grey pledged to reform an electoral system that had seen few changes since the fifteenth century, in which small boroughs such as Old Sarum, with seven voters, elected two members while Manchester and Birmingham elected none. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-act-1832">Reform Act 1832</a> refashioned it. His popularity was eroded by the Reform Crisis.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, William signed a new constitution in 1833 that empowered the middle class and expanded the role of the parliament; his brother Ernest Augustus later revoked it. He supported Belgian independence and favoured Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold of Saxe-Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> for its throne. In 1834 he became the last British sovereign to choose a prime minister against the will of Parliament, when he disliked the policies of Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>'s government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>William died on 20 June 1837 with no surviving legitimate children. The British throne passed to his niece Victoria, but under Salic Law a woman could not rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, so that throne went to his brother Ernest Augustus. His death ended the personal union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Hanover that had lasted since 1714. The main beneficiaries of his will were his eight surviving children by Mrs Jordan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>William IV of the United Kingdom</b> was the last monarch to dismiss a prime minister, Lord Melbourne, in 1834, and Queen Victoria’s personal power waned during her reign.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>It continued in personal union with Great Britain under George III, George IV and <b>William IV</b>, governed by a viceroy.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Sixth Frontier War of 1834-1836, the Cape governor Sir Benjamin D'Urban proclaimed in May 1835 that the territory between the Keiskamma and Kei rivers was British, naming it after Queen Adelaide, wife of <b>William IV</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-queen-adelaide">Province of Queen Adelaide</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Buckingham Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Windsor Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.5,-0.14&amp;year=1811" aria-label="Buckingham Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William IV of the United Kingdom"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.5,-0.14&amp;year=1811">What was here in 1811? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
